KUF port M13: record guest memory uploads at the start of the submission when no command of the submission
has accessed their pages yet. In place, each upload ends the render pass and adds two whole-buffer pipeline
barriers (shader reads -> transfer, transfer -> reads) that drain the GPU; the game uploads a few hundred times a
frame. The upload pages accessed by the submission (requested ranges, GPU-written ranges) are tracked per page;
uploads that touch an accessed page stay in place. --vulkan_hoist_shared_memory_uploads=false restores the old
path. --gpu_timestamp_stats reports the uploads per submission and per second.

KUF port M14: a page the CPU invalidated is uploaded again only when its contents changed. Write protection
invalidates a window of pages around each CPU write, and during play 99% of the pages uploaded again were byte for
byte what the GPU already had (1.5-3 GB/s of uploads at 4K). Pages uploaded more than once keep a copy of what was
last uploaded (up to --vulkan_upload_skip_unchanged_mb, 128 by default; 0 turns this off); a page equal to its copy
is protected again without an upload. Uploads of such pages are made from the copy, so it always equals the
buffer's contents, and every GPU write to a page (resolves, memory export) drops its copy.
